<div class="content">
    <h1>Teaching guide: reading</h1>
    <p class="content__intro">The reading lessons follow the method of Siegfried Engelmann's <em>Teach Your Child to Read in 100 Easy Lessons</em> and the Direct Instruction reading programs behind it. You read the script, the child answers, and you mark each answer. This guide explains the parts of a reading lesson and the few habits that make them work. The <a href="/guide/">math guide</a> covers the screen, the marks, and the corrections that both subjects share.</p>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">How the program is built</h2>
      <p>Reading is taught as a set of skills, each with its own track, each returning lesson after lesson. <a href="/reading-1/">Reading 1</a> has a hundred lessons and teaches forty-odd sounds, blending by ear, sounding out, reading the fast way, the common words that do not follow their sounds, and the first sentences and stories. <a href="/reading-2/">Reading 2</a> has fifty lessons in ordinary letters: blends, the sounds spelled with two or three letters, silent letters, endings, longer stories, and reading against the clock.</p>
      <p>A new sound or skill is taught in full, firmed for the next two lessons, and then reviewed at widening intervals. Every ten lessons a <strong>mastery test</strong> checks each skill, and from lesson 50 the test ends with a passage the child reads alone. The <a href="/placement/reading/">placement test</a> finds the lesson to begin with.</p>
      <p>The order of the sounds, the words, and the stories came from Open Phonics, and the program keeps them. The <a href="/sounds/">Sounds</a> page lists every sound with its lesson; the <a href="/words/">word list</a> gives every word; the <a href="/stories/">Stories</a> page holds the thirty stories.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">The child's letters</h2>
      <p>In Reading 1 the letters carry marks that make the sounds plain. A <strong>bar</strong> under two letters means they make one sound: <em>th</em>, <em>sh</em>, <em>ar</em>. A <strong>line</strong> over a vowel means its long sound, the one that says its name. A <strong>small letter</strong> is silent; the child does not say it. The marks fade as the child gains skill: the lines and the small letters go after lesson 74, the bars after lesson 90, and Reading 2 is in ordinary letters from the start.</p>
      <p>A word a child cannot sound out, such as <em>was</em> or <em>said</em>, is taught as a whole word and shown in plain letters. The <a href="/words/">word list</a> marks these.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">The dot</h2>
      <p>Under a word for sounding out is an arrow with a red dot. The dot takes the place of your finger under the page. <strong>Press and hold the Dot button</strong>, at the bottom of the screen, to move the dot under the next sound and keep it there. The child says that sound as long as the dot rests. Let go, and the dot stays; press again, and it moves on. The dot moves only when you move it, so the pace is yours.</p>
      <ul class="guide-tips">
        <li><strong>Hold</strong> means a continuous sound: hold the button about two seconds while the child says mmm or aaa, with no stop between one sound and the next.</li>
        <li><strong>Tap</strong> means a stop sound: touch the button and let go at once. The child says t or d short, with no "uh" after it.</li>
        <li><strong>Sweep</strong> means the fast way: one quick tap sends the dot under the whole word, and the child says the word at once.</li>
        <li>In a sentence or a story the dot moves from word to word. Press for each word as the child reads it.</li>
        <li>On a keyboard, hold the space bar to hold the dot, and press D to move it one sound on.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">The parts of a lesson</h2>
      <div class="guide-cards">
        <div class="guide-card"><h3 class="guide-card__title">Sounds</h3><p>Every lesson begins with sounds: a new one every lesson or two, then a review. Say each sound as it is said in a word, never as a letter name. Hold a continuous sound; say a stop sound short.</p></div>
        <div class="guide-card"><h3 class="guide-card__title">Say it fast</h3><p>You say a word slowly, in parts, mmm...at, and the child says it fast: mat. Nothing is on the screen. Pause between the parts, then drop your hand as the signal.</p></div>
        <div class="guide-card"><h3 class="guide-card__title">Say the sounds</h3><p>The reverse: you say a word, and the child says it slowly, one sound at a time. Hold up a finger for each sound.</p></div>
        <div class="guide-card"><h3 class="guide-card__title">Rhyming</h3><p>"Rhymes with at. It starts with mmm." The child says mat. Rhyming shows the child that words are made of parts that can change.</p></div>
        <div class="guide-card"><h3 class="guide-card__title">Sounding out</h3><p>The child says each sound as the dot rests under it, with no stop between sounds, mmmaaat, and then says the word fast: mat. Until the child says it fast, the word is not read.</p></div>
        <div class="guide-card"><h3 class="guide-card__title">The fast way</h3><p>From lesson 20, words taught earlier are read without sounding out. The dot sweeps; the child says the word. If the child stumbles, sound it out together, then read it the fast way again.</p></div>
        <div class="guide-card"><h3 class="guide-card__title">Words read whole</h3><p>"This word is was. What word?" The child learns the word as a whole and meets it often after.</p></div>
        <div class="guide-card"><h3 class="guide-card__title">Sentences and stories</h3><p>A sentence is read word by word first, each word sounded out and said fast, then the whole sentence the fast way. A story is read line by line, and questions follow. In Reading 2 the lines are read the fast way.</p></div>
        <div class="guide-card"><h3 class="guide-card__title">Checkouts</h3><p>From lesson 40, every fifth lesson ends with a passage the child reads alone while the clock runs. Tap Start as the child begins, Error for each word missed, Next at the end of each line, and Stop at the end. Words a minute and errors go to Records.</p></div>
        <div class="guide-card"><h3 class="guide-card__title">On your own</h3><p>A few words or lines at the end, read alone with no help, marked but not corrected. They show what the child can do without you.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">Marking and correcting</h2>
      <p>Mark every answer <strong>Firm</strong> or <strong>Error</strong>. An answer is firm when it is right and comes without hesitation: a sound held or said short as it should be, a word sounded out without stopping between sounds and then said fast, a line read without a stumble. Mark an error at once, and the panel shows the correction for that item: you say the answer, say it with the child, and ask again. The item comes back a little later in the lesson, and once more at the end.</p>
      <ul class="guide-tips">
        <li><strong>A letter name is an error.</strong> If the child says "em" for m, correct with the sound: "My turn: mmm."</li>
        <li><strong>A stop between sounds is an error.</strong> If the child says mmm, aaa, t with pauses, model the word again with the sounds run together.</li>
        <li><strong>Sounding out is not reading.</strong> After the sounds, always ask the child to say it fast. The fast word is the answer.</li>
        <li><strong>In a story, correct the word, then the line.</strong> If a word is missed, sound it out together, say it fast, and read the line again from its start.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">Praise</h2>
      <p>Confirm a right answer with the answer itself: "Yes, mmm." "Yes, mat." That is the praise. It tells the child exactly what was right, and it keeps the lesson moving. The program never adds points, stars, or prizes, and it never asks you to.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">Teaching two children</h2>
      <p>Two children can take a reading lesson together. Open More and choose Teach two children. Sounds, say it fast, and words are answered in unison, and each child takes one turn alone at the end of each exercise. The lines of a sentence or a story, and the questions after it, go to one child at a time, in turn, as a Direct Instruction group gives individual turns in reading. A checkout is read by each child in turn, and each gets a record. The marks, the On your own items, and the results are kept for each child.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">Tests and placement</h2>
      <p>A mastery test comes after every tenth lesson. It is given without help, one part for each skill, and from lesson 50 it ends with a passage read against the clock. A part not passed names the exercises to teach again, and that part alone can be given again. The next lesson waits until the test is passed, or until you mark it passed yourself on the results screen.</p>
      <p>The <a href="/placement/reading/">placement test</a> begins by checking that the child can say a sound after you and say a word fast from its parts, then checks each span of twenty lessons until one is not passed, and names the lesson to begin with. A child who has finished Reading 1, or a hundred lessons like it, can start with Reading 2's sections.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">Practice</h2>
      <p>Under Practice, <strong>Quick sounds</strong> gives twelve sounds the child has been taught, missed sounds first, and keeps a record of each. <strong>Stories</strong> lists the thirty stories, with the lesson each becomes readable at, to read again at any time. Each reading track has a practice set drawn from the lessons the child has finished.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-section">
      <h2 class="guide-section__title">When a child struggles</h2>
      <ul class="guide-tips">
        <li><strong>Go slower, not faster.</strong> Hold the dot longer. Model more. Say it with the child before asking alone.</li>
        <li><strong>Repeat the lesson.</strong> If more than one answer in four needed correcting, the finish screen says so. Teach the lesson again next time. This is normal.</li>
        <li><strong>Check the sounds.</strong> Most trouble with words is trouble with a sound. The sound chart on Records shows which sounds are not yet firm; Quick sounds brings them up first.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep the sittings short.</strong> One lesson a day, four or five days a week, is enough. Stop while the child is still doing well.</li>
        <li><strong>Do not teach letter names.</strong> Names come later, on their own. For now the letters have sounds.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
</div>
